WEBVTT

NOTE Episode: The Ride He Chose

1
00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:25.318
<v Old Timber>Ah, there is our song. Find a place by the pond. Bongo the bear cub is testing Gentle Log while Shelly the tortoise watches.</v>

2
00:00:25.668 --> 00:00:32.175
<v Old Timber>Garingo the parrot has appointed himself launch announcer, and Fern the fawn is studying the log.</v>

3
00:00:32.525 --> 00:00:41.972
<v Bongo>First you sit on the log. Then Shelly pushes. Then I shout, Wheee! Safety plan complete.</v>

4
00:00:42.322 --> 00:00:50.132
<v Garingo>BEHOLD! The Gentle Log launches at the speed of destiny, provided somebody eventually pushes it.</v>

5
00:00:50.482 --> 00:00:53.077
<v Fern>Um... it has not moved yet.</v>

6
00:00:53.427 --> 00:00:56.347
<v Shelly>The safety plan is mostly shouting.</v>

7
00:00:56.697 --> 00:01:05.015
<v Old Timber>Today, Jigstar has brought a true holiday story about fear, wise choices, and courage that was not the biggest thing in the park.</v>

8
00:01:05.365 --> 00:01:12.525
<v Old Timber>God can strengthen a heart without removing every fear. Listen close; the brave part may surprise you.</v>

9
00:01:13.225 --> 00:01:20.545
<v Old Timber>Jigstar, the human boy, came down the path with a folded park map and an adventure still making noise inside him.</v>

10
00:01:20.895 --> 00:01:33.045
<v Jigstar>Can I ask you something? Why do park maps print snack stands and sky-high roller coasters the same size? That map was withholding information.</v>

11
00:01:33.395 --> 00:01:36.795
<v Bongo>Snack stands should be much bigger on maps.</v>

12
00:01:37.145 --> 00:01:39.795
<v Shelly>Finally, useful map-making.</v>

13
00:01:40.145 --> 00:01:52.825
<v Bongo>Because the tallest ride is obviously the bravest ride. I would choose the biggest one, lift both paws, and possibly become mayor of the sky.</v>

14
00:01:53.175 --> 00:01:56.095
<v Shelly>The sky has not requested a mayor.</v>

15
00:01:56.445 --> 00:02:04.375
<v Garingo>As I was SAYING, courage deserves altitude, banners, and a speech from the newly elected mayor.</v>

16
00:02:04.725 --> 00:02:16.998
<v Jigstar>That was nearly my problem, mate. I thought brave meant choosing the tallest, fastest ride, and I wanted no part of that rule.</v>

17
00:02:17.348 --> 00:02:23.866
<v Bongo>How many rides did you say no to? One? Two? A small parade of no?</v>

18
00:02:24.216 --> 00:02:26.733
<v Bongo>I can carry a small parade.</v>

19
00:02:27.083 --> 00:02:37.309
<v Jigstar>Several. I had reasons for every one. Strong reasons. Organised reasons. Some of them had sub-reasons.</v>

20
00:02:37.659 --> 00:02:42.734
<v Fern>Were all the reasons about the rides, or was one about what somebody might think of you?</v>

21
00:02:43.084 --> 00:02:45.440
<v Shelly>Fear, with a filing system.</v>

22
00:02:45.790 --> 00:02:47.559
<v Shelly>A reason with pockets.</v>

23
00:02:47.909 --> 00:02:55.069
<v Bongo>Were the reasons in ABC order? My reasons usually arrive in a heap and sit on the useful reason.</v>

24
00:02:55.419 --> 00:03:05.317
<v Jigstar>Height, speed, loops, suspicious tunnels, and whether the ride name sounded like a thunderstorm.</v>

25
00:03:05.667 --> 00:03:08.986
<v Bongo>My ride is called Gentle Log.</v>

26
00:03:09.336 --> 00:03:11.216
<v Shelly>Very tidy fear.</v>

27
00:03:11.566 --> 00:03:23.638
<v Jigstar>That was the reason behind the reasons. I worried Dad might think I had wasted the holiday, or that I had not tried hard enough.</v>

28
00:03:23.988 --> 00:03:27.138
<v Garingo>That fear makes more noise than a lift chain.</v>

29
00:03:27.488 --> 00:03:37.687
<v Bongo>I do that before a creek jump. I explain the mud, the wind, the leaf angle, and somebody else's suspicious jumping face.</v>

30
00:03:38.037 --> 00:03:42.797
<v Fern>The reasons can be true and still hide the smallest fear underneath.</v>

31
00:03:43.147 --> 00:03:53.347
<v Bongo>For the record, my log ride has no loops, tunnels, or weather. It also has no movement, because Shelly has declined the pushing department.</v>

32
00:03:53.697 --> 00:03:56.297
<v Shelly>The pushing department has standards.</v>

33
00:03:56.647 --> 00:04:00.842
<v Jigstar>Exactly. Let me tell you what happened.</v>

34
00:04:01.545 --> 00:04:10.545
<v Old Timber>As Jigstar spoke, the pond made room for bright flags, humming rails, busy paths, and a tower poking the afternoon.</v>

35
00:04:10.895 --> 00:04:22.327
<v Jigstar>We stayed at a huge resort where roller coasters passed the breakfast window. One carriage sailed by like it had an appointment with the clouds.</v>

36
00:04:22.677 --> 00:04:25.836
<v Bongo>Breakfast with sky appointments. Fancy.</v>

37
00:04:26.186 --> 00:04:27.966
<v Shelly>The cereal was on time.</v>

38
00:04:28.316 --> 00:04:41.602
<v Jigstar>The park smelled like warm popcorn, sun cream, and hot pavement. Somewhere above us, a lift chain began its slow metal climb.</v>

39
00:04:41.952 --> 00:04:47.112
<v Bongo>Warm popcorn is an excellent smell and a brave lunch.</v>

40
00:04:47.462 --> 00:04:53.178
<v Old Timber>Some rides spun gently. Others seemed named by someone shouting into a thunderstorm.</v>

41
00:04:53.528 --> 00:04:56.048
<v Bongo>Gentle Log was named in a whisper.</v>

42
00:04:56.398 --> 00:04:57.425
<v Shelly>By the log.</v>

43
00:04:57.775 --> 00:05:09.215
<v Jigstar>I liked the river boats, the slow high wheel, and especially the bench by the snack stand. Five stars. Very steady. Zero drops.</v>

44
00:05:09.565 --> 00:05:11.401
<v Bongo>Five stars for the bench.</v>

45
00:05:11.751 --> 00:05:17.193
<v Old Timber>Dad enjoyed the calm rides too. A peaceful moment never became a campaign.</v>

46
00:05:17.543 --> 00:05:26.076
<v Jigstar>The first coaster looked like a hurried shoelace and went upside down, so I said no.</v>

47
00:05:26.426 --> 00:05:29.986
<v Shelly>Shoelaces should stay off the transport committee.</v>

48
00:05:30.336 --> 00:05:41.715
<v Jigstar>The second vanished into a tunnel. I said no because rides keep surprises in tunnels, and I prefer surprises to submit a form.</v>

49
00:05:42.065 --> 00:05:45.384
<v Bongo>Forms need clipboards. I can help.</v>

50
00:05:45.734 --> 00:05:53.573
<v Jigstar>The third blew my park map into my face. I said no because it had already attacked the paperwork.</v>

51
00:05:53.923 --> 00:05:55.383
<v Shelly>Evidence entered.</v>

52
00:05:55.733 --> 00:05:57.903
<v Bongo>The map was a witness.</v>

53
00:05:58.253 --> 00:06:04.533
<v Old Timber>Dad listened. He did not laugh, pull Jigstar toward a queue, or call the fear silly.</v>

54
00:06:04.883 --> 00:06:11.003
<v Fern>After each firm no, did Dad let it stand before asking whether you wanted help with a different choice?</v>

55
00:06:11.353 --> 00:06:20.059
<v Jigstar>He let each no stand. Later he asked whether I wanted help thinking about a different ride.</v>

56
00:06:20.409 --> 00:06:23.552
<v Bongo>So he offered help, not a decision?</v>

57
00:06:23.902 --> 00:06:38.000
<v Jigstar>Exactly. We took a snack break and sorted firm boundaries from things that only felt difficult because they were new.</v>

58
00:06:38.350 --> 00:06:43.110
<v Fern>Support helped you hear your own answer. Pressure would have drowned it out.</v>

59
00:06:43.460 --> 00:06:48.780
<v Shelly>The extreme ride stayed a no. Difficult did not always mean wrong.</v>

60
00:06:49.130 --> 00:06:54.479
<v Garingo>Then the grandest act was walking past the grandest ride.</v>

61
00:06:54.829 --> 00:07:05.407
<v Jigstar>Dad said that no was allowed. Then he asked whether there was one difficult ride I could honestly choose for myself.</v>

62
00:07:05.757 --> 00:07:14.470
<v Garingo>THE TOWER OF ABSOLUTELY NOT. THE BENCH OF GLORIOUS PEACE. And one difficult hill of maybe-I-can.</v>

63
00:07:14.820 --> 00:07:19.177
<v Shelly>The names need work. The choices do not.</v>

64
00:07:19.527 --> 00:07:30.042
<v Bongo>I need objects. Pine cone: extreme no. Flat stone: easy yes. Acorn: difficult yes that might be yours.</v>

65
00:07:30.392 --> 00:07:36.272
<v Fern>The acorn is not the biggest one. It is the one Jigstar is still thinking about.</v>

66
00:07:36.622 --> 00:07:42.425
<v Garingo>A modest acorn carries the entire drama. I respect its range.</v>

67
00:07:42.775 --> 00:07:47.576
<v Bongo>So the question stopped being, Can I prove I am never scared?</v>

68
00:07:47.926 --> 00:07:54.216
<v Old Timber>They walked past the extreme ride. Jigstar kept his wise no, and Dad respected it.</v>

69
00:07:54.566 --> 00:08:05.856
<v Jigstar>Exactly. Keeping that no made the rest clearer. I was looking for one ride that matched the courage I actually had.</v>

70
00:08:06.206 --> 00:08:09.032
<v Shelly>What made the other coaster a possible yes?</v>

71
00:08:09.382 --> 00:08:23.140
<v Jigstar>It had one high climb, one deep swoop, and no upside-down part. We watched a full trip, checked the restraints, and read the rules.</v>

72
00:08:23.490 --> 00:08:27.370
<v Fern>You gave the fear facts, so it had less guessing to feed on.</v>

73
00:08:27.720 --> 00:08:33.222
<v Old Timber>Dad answered each question, then left the choice where it belonged: with Jigstar.</v>

74
00:08:33.572 --> 00:08:41.894
<v Jigstar>I chose that one. My voice came out steady enough. My knees joined a drum group.</v>

75
00:08:42.606 --> 00:08:50.706
<v Jigstar>The queue moved with terrible efficiency for a line that did not know I might need another forty minutes.</v>

76
00:08:51.056 --> 00:08:57.416
<v Old Timber>The queue zigzagged beneath a shaded roof, showing the platform at one turn and hiding it at the next.</v>

77
00:08:57.766 --> 00:09:09.445
<v Jigstar>At one corner, I was riding. At the next, I was becoming a bench enthusiast. Then I remembered I already was one.</v>

78
00:09:09.795 --> 00:09:12.394
<v Bongo>You changed your mind at every corner?</v>

79
00:09:12.744 --> 00:09:21.185
<v Garingo>A heroic zigzag of maybe! Left turn: rider. Right turn: fancy bench.</v>

80
00:09:21.535 --> 00:09:24.215
<v Fern>He was deciding again at every corner.</v>

81
00:09:24.565 --> 00:09:32.445
<v Old Timber>Dad stayed beside him. He reminded Jigstar that he could still change his mind before boarding, and that the choice belonged to him.</v>

82
00:09:32.795 --> 00:09:44.280
<v Jigstar>Knowing I could leave mattered. Every step forward was still my choice, not a trap made by the steps behind me.</v>

83
00:09:44.630 --> 00:09:53.105
<v Jigstar>I took one slow breath and asked God for strength to make a wise choice, not to make me look fearless.</v>

84
00:09:53.455 --> 00:10:01.575
<v Old Timber>The coaster stayed exactly its size. Jigstar's heart still thumped. But the next choice became clear enough to hold.</v>

85
00:10:01.925 --> 00:10:06.667
<v Jigstar>I wanted my fear to leave first. It did not.</v>

86
00:10:07.017 --> 00:10:10.177
<v Old Timber>His hands felt cold, even in the warm air.</v>

87
00:10:10.527 --> 00:10:21.552
<v Jigstar>For a moment, I thought being scared meant my yes was not real. I also feared I would let Dad down if I left.</v>

88
00:10:21.902 --> 00:10:27.300
<v Fern>Fear said you would let Dad down. Dad had stayed kind through every no.</v>

89
00:10:27.650 --> 00:10:33.210
<v Shelly>But you still knew which ride was your no, and which one was your yes.</v>

90
00:10:33.560 --> 00:10:38.906
<v Garingo>No fanfare, then. The choice is brave before anybody cheers.</v>

91
00:10:39.256 --> 00:10:46.654
<v Old Timber>The gate opened. Jigstar breathed in, looked at Dad, and stepped into the carriage because he had chosen to.</v>

92
00:10:47.004 --> 00:10:55.615
<v Jigstar>The lap bar clicked. I asked it whether that was its final answer. The lap bar remained professional.</v>

93
00:10:55.965 --> 00:10:58.085
<v Bongo>The lap bar did not answer?</v>

94
00:10:58.435 --> 00:11:07.595
<v Old Timber>Then the carriage rolled forward and caught the lift chain: clack, click, clack, climbing one careful notch at a time.</v>

95
00:11:08.299 --> 00:11:15.655
<v Jigstar>At the bottom I had six reasons to get off. At the top, the ride had misplaced the bottom.</v>

96
00:11:16.005 --> 00:11:23.645
<v Old Timber>From the top, the paths looked like ribbons. Far below, the breakfast window continued its important cereal work.</v>

97
00:11:23.995 --> 00:11:37.073
<v Jigstar>I could see the calm rides, the snack stand, and the extreme coaster I had not chosen. My no and my yes were both still mine.</v>

98
00:11:37.423 --> 00:11:47.083
<v Old Timber>The car tipped over the first hill. Wind rushed past, wheels sang on rail, and Jigstar's scared sound became a surprised laugh.</v>

99
00:11:47.433 --> 00:12:01.224
<v Jigstar>We swooped, climbed, and curved around the tower. I held the bar and laughed so hard my organised reasons flew out of their pockets.</v>

100
00:12:01.574 --> 00:12:03.614
<v Shelly>Very untidy fear.</v>

101
00:12:03.964 --> 00:12:10.055
<v Garingo>I prepared a twelve-part victory speech. I am placing it quietly under my wing.</v>

102
00:12:10.405 --> 00:12:18.285
<v Old Timber>There was one last curve, a bright rush beneath the flags, and then the brakes softened the carriage back toward the platform.</v>

103
00:12:18.635 --> 00:12:27.045
<v Old Timber>When the carriage slowed at the platform, Dad did not say, I told you so. He smiled and let Jigstar name what had happened.</v>

104
00:12:27.395 --> 00:12:36.922
<v Jigstar>I told him I had been brave while I was scared. I also told him I loved that ride. Both things were true.</v>

105
00:12:37.272 --> 00:12:44.612
<v Garingo>If you had stepped away at the gate, Dad's kindness would still have come home with you. The ride could not earn it.</v>

106
00:12:44.962 --> 00:12:49.802
<v Shelly>Enjoying one yes did not turn every no into a mistake.</v>

107
00:12:50.152 --> 00:13:00.791
<v Jigstar>Exactly. Another child might choose differently, and I might choose differently another day. My extreme-ride no still counted.</v>

108
00:13:01.141 --> 00:13:07.626
<v Old Timber>They had a cold drink and a quiet sit. The bench, Jigstar reported, performed magnificently.</v>

109
00:13:07.976 --> 00:13:15.098
<v Jigstar>I circled my ride on the map and left the extreme one outside the circle.</v>

110
00:13:15.448 --> 00:13:20.767
<v Shelly>So the map became a record of what you chose, not a list of what you failed?</v>

111
00:13:21.117 --> 00:13:22.806
<v Fern>That is a kinder map.</v>

112
00:13:23.156 --> 00:13:27.596
<v Bongo>Did you bring the map? I would like to inspect the official circle.</v>

113
00:13:27.946 --> 00:13:37.037
<v Jigstar>Right here, mate. One ride circled. Three snack stands also circled for separate research reasons.</v>

114
00:13:37.387 --> 00:13:40.097
<v Shelly>The map is sharing more information now.</v>

115
00:13:40.447 --> 00:13:51.127
<v Old Timber>Back at the pond, Bongo had become unusually quiet. Unusually quiet for Bongo is still louder than a spoon falling into porridge, but we all noticed.</v>

116
00:13:51.477 --> 00:13:54.955
<v Bongo>I think my mayor-of-the-sky rule was wrong.</v>

117
00:13:55.305 --> 00:13:58.145
<v Shelly>The sky accepts your step-down.</v>

118
00:13:58.495 --> 00:14:01.704
<v Garingo>Courage is not elected by altitude.</v>

119
00:14:02.054 --> 00:14:06.414
<v Bongo>So the biggest ride never got to decide what brave meant?</v>

120
00:14:06.764 --> 00:14:13.836
<v Jigstar>Exactly, mate. I kept my no and chose a yes I could own.</v>

121
00:14:14.545 --> 00:14:17.128
<v Old Timber>What was the bravest thing Jigstar did?</v>

122
00:14:17.478 --> 00:14:24.078
<v Shelly>He listened closely enough to tell fear from a boundary, and a boundary from a choice he was ready to try.</v>

123
00:14:24.428 --> 00:14:28.387
<v Fern>He let Dad encourage him without handing Dad the choice.</v>

124
00:14:28.737 --> 00:14:34.357
<v Garingo>And he kept the brave no beside the brave yes. No trumpet or parade required.</v>

125
00:14:34.707 --> 00:14:35.626
<v Old Timber>Mm.</v>

126
00:14:35.976 --> 00:14:46.576
<v Old Timber>One of David's songs in the Bible says, in Psalm 31, verse 24: Be of good courage, and he shall strengthen your heart, all ye that hope in the LORD.</v>

127
00:14:46.926 --> 00:14:56.156
<v Jigstar>All ye means all of us who hope in God. It says strengthen your heart, not that your knees must stop drumming first.</v>

128
00:14:56.506 --> 00:15:01.094
<v Old Timber>Mm. We can ask God for strength for the wise choice in front of us.</v>

129
00:15:01.795 --> 00:15:09.030
<v Old Timber>Professor Hoot, the most certain owl in Shiloh Creek, settled on his lecture perch with one wing already raised.</v>

130
00:15:09.380 --> 00:15:16.718
<v Professor Hoot>A fact that is completely true: a roller-coaster car stores potential energy as it is pulled up the first hill.</v>

131
00:15:17.068 --> 00:15:30.745
<v Professor Hoot>Potential energy means stored energy, waiting to become motion. On the way down, gravity changes much of it into kinetic energy, which simply means the energy of moving.</v>

132
00:15:31.095 --> 00:15:40.493
<v Professor Hoot>The slow click-click climb is therefore not wasting time. It is storing the swoop. A fact, children, proven beyond dispute.</v>

133
00:15:40.843 --> 00:15:44.083
<v Shelly>Bongo has been storing a swoop in that log.</v>

134
00:15:44.433 --> 00:15:47.579
<v Bongo>It is currently one hundred percent potential.</v>

135
00:15:53.429 --> 00:16:05.069
<v Old Timber>Then out from the reeds came the Mudpuppy brothers, Splish, Splash, and Splosh, three frogs and one chorus of confusion, splashing in for Mudpuppy Minutes.</v>

136
00:16:05.419 --> 00:16:09.432
<v Splosh>Today on Mudpuppy Minutes: why do socks disappear in the wash?</v>

137
00:16:09.782 --> 00:16:13.262
<v Splish>They become hand puppets and leave to pursue theatre.</v>

138
00:16:13.612 --> 00:16:22.372
<v Splash>No, louder! The washing machine eats one sock from every pair because it is building a very small scarf.</v>

139
00:16:22.722 --> 00:16:28.755
<v Splosh>Incorrect. Socks hide inside larger clothes or slip behind the machine.</v>

140
00:16:29.105 --> 00:16:32.415
<v Splish>Oh no, no. That cannot be right. Too ordinary.</v>

141
00:16:32.765 --> 00:16:35.621
<v Splash>Maybe the sock is still inside the trouser leg.</v>

142
00:16:35.971 --> 00:16:39.131
<v Splish>That is the trouser's idea. The sock is innocent.</v>

143
00:16:39.481 --> 00:16:43.071
<v Splash>Then the scarf must be for the washing machine's winter concert.</v>

144
00:16:43.421 --> 00:16:50.820
<v Splosh>Nah. Mudpuppies, mudpuppies, we have solved it. Missing socks are actors wearing laundry scarves.</v>

145
00:16:51.170 --> 00:16:55.370
<v Splish>If socks are actors, why do they perform only when I need to leave the house?</v>

146
00:16:55.720 --> 00:16:59.130
<v Splash>Because laundry theatre understands suspense.</v>

147
00:16:59.480 --> 00:17:01.359
<v Splosh>Suspense is expensive.</v>

148
00:17:01.709 --> 00:17:03.109
<v Splish>Charge it to the scarf.</v>

149
00:17:03.459 --> 00:17:13.739
<v Old Timber>A missing sock is usually tucked inside another piece of clothing or dropped beside the machine. Socks do not leave for the theatre, though some of them are dramatic.</v>

150
00:17:14.442 --> 00:17:23.042
<v Old Timber>If something difficult stands in front of you today, little one, you do not have to prove yourself with the biggest choice. Talk with a trusted grown-up.</v>

151
00:17:23.392 --> 00:17:29.832
<v Old Timber>Keep the honest no you need, and ask God for strength to take the honest yes you are ready to choose.</v>

152
00:17:30.182 --> 00:17:35.637
<v Jigstar>And if your knees start a drum group, they can come too.</v>

153
00:17:35.987 --> 00:17:43.146
<v Old Timber>Jigstar folded his map, waved to the pond, and headed home with one careful circle still showing on the page.</v>

154
00:17:43.496 --> 00:17:49.536
<v Old Timber>Until the next brave little sound brings us together, this is Old Timber at Shiloh Creek.</v>

155
00:17:49.886 --> 00:17:53.046
<v Ranger Ray>See you next time, somewhere in Shiloh Creek.</v>
